        <title type="html"><![CDATA[fix: mention that pre doesn't strip leading '\n' in XML]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/42488</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/42488"/>
        <updated>2025-12-21T13:31:23.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/34214253?u=804c953fcb346d61e2ec21689f530dcd09810117&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto">That is a feature of the HTML syntax parsing rules, not the HTML abstract language itself, hence does not apply when using the XML syntax of HTML (also known as XHTML).</p>
<p dir="auto">The HTML standard notes this as:</p>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto">In the HTML syntax, a leading newline character immediately following the pre element start tag is stripped.</p>
</blockquote>]]></content>
